================================================================================
Third-party components and data
================================================================================

Riprap depends on third-party open-source software and public-record
datasets. Each retains its own upstream licence; the list below records
attribution where required.

Models:
  Riprap ships no model weights. A briefing needs no model. Two kinds
  are optional:

  - An LLM behind an OpenAI-compatible endpoint the operator
    configures, for example IBM Granite 4.1 8B (Apache 2.0).
  - Two experimental models, fine-tuned by the author. Their output is
    labelled experimental wherever it appears (docs/MODELS.md). One is
    published under Apache 2.0 and downloaded from Hugging Face at a
    pinned commit when its optional extra is installed:
      msradam/Granite-TTM-r2-Battery-Surge, a fine-tune of IBM's
        Granite TimeSeries TTM r2 (Apache 2.0).
    Training and evaluation code: github.com/msradam/riprap-models.
    (msradam/Prithvi-EO-2.0-NYC-Pluvial, a fine-tune of NASA and IBM's
    Prithvi-EO 2.0, was a third until it was retired from the app in
    October 2026; the scripts that tested it still download it.)
    The other, the NYC land-cover model, is IBM and ESA's TerraMind 1.0
    base (Apache 2.0) fine-tuned in this repository
    (scripts/train_cover.py) on NYC Land Cover 2017; its weights are
    not published, and the app reads its saved maps (data/eo/).

Public-record data sources (no commercial APIs):
  Each pebble manifest's provenance block records its source's licence,
  URL and vintage; docs/DATA-SOURCES.md lists the NYC sources.
  - FEMA National Flood Hazard Layer, effective and preliminary
    (U.S. public domain)
  - National Weather Service alerts, observations and National Water
    Prediction Service water-level forecasts (U.S. public domain)
  - NOAA CO-OPS water levels and tide predictions (U.S. public domain)
  - USGS Water Data, the Short-Term Network Hurricane Ida high-water
    marks and the 3DEP elevation model (U.S. public domain)
  - NYC Open Data: Sandy Inundation Zone, DEP Stormwater Flood Maps,
    311 Service Requests, DOB Permit Issuance, school locations, NYCHA
    developments, 2020 Neighborhood Tabulation Areas (NYC Open Data
    Terms of Use)
  - NYC Department of City Planning, Community District Profiles,
    floodplain indicators (NYC Open Data Terms of Use)
  - New York State open data: MTA subway entrances and exits, Health
    Facility General Information (the publishers' open data terms)
  - FloodNet (New York University and The City University of New York)
    street flood sensor data, read from the FloodNet Data API at the
    time of each briefing (CC BY-NC-SA 4.0,
    https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/, under FloodNet's
    Data Access License Agreement). Cite: Mydlarz et al. (2024), Water
    Resources Research 60, e2023WR036806,
    https://doi.org/10.1029/2023WR036806; Silverman et al. (2022), Water
    Research, 118648, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.watres.2022.118648.
    See "FloodNet-derived content" below.
  - New York City Panel on Climate Change 2024 Report, NPCC4, Annals of
    the New York Academy of Sciences vol. 1539 (CC BY-NC 4.0)
  - Experimental deployments: Chicago Data Portal and Seattle Open Data
    311 service requests (each portal's terms of use), SeeClickFix
    public API for Albany, NY

FloodNet-derived content:
  The Apache-2.0 licence above covers Riprap's code. It does not cover
  FloodNet data or the content Riprap derives from it: the FloodNet
  sentences and figures in a briefing, in the JSON API, in MCP tool
  results and in the gallery snapshots (web/sveltekit/src/lib/gallery/).
  That content is licensed CC BY-NC-SA 4.0: non-commercial use only,
  with the attribution above, and under the same licence. Each response
  that contains it says so in its floodnet citation record (license,
  license_url, attribution, references, license_notice, retrieved_at).
  The script that scores models against FloodNet events
  (scripts/score_water_floodnet.py) writes the event rows to a
  git-ignored folder and commits aggregate results only.
  Riprap is independent of FloodNet, New York University and The City
  University of New York, and is not endorsed by them.

Earth-observation data:
  - Contains modified Copernicus Sentinel data (Sentinel-2 L2A, 2017 to
    2026), accessed through Microsoft Planetary Computer (Copernicus
    Sentinel data terms), read by the land-cover model
  - Landsat 8 and 9 Collection 2 Level 2 surface temperature, courtesy
    of the U.S. Geological Survey (public domain), accessed through
    Microsoft Planetary Computer: data/heat/surface_temp.tif and the map
    overlay web/sveltekit/static/heat/surface.png

Heat records (New York City):
  - Heat Vulnerability Index and heat stress emergency department
    visits, NYC Department of Health and Mental Hygiene, Environment
    and Health Data Portal (github.com/nychealth/EHDP-data, Apache 2.0;
    visits from New York State SPARCS): data/heat/dohmh_heat.json
  - Daily station records, NOAA Regional Climate Centers, ACIS (U.S.
    public domain)
  - Forecasts, observations and alerts, National Weather Service (U.S.
    public domain)
  - NPCC4 extreme heat projections, Braneon et al. 2024, Annals of the
    New York Academy of Sciences 1539, doi:10.1111/nyas.15116 (CC BY-NC
    4.0), Table 4, quoted with citation
  - NYC Parks Spray Showers and Pools, NYC Open Data ckaz-6gaa and
    y5rm-wagw (NYC Open Data Terms of Use): data/heat/parks_cooling.json
  - NYC Land Cover 2017, 6 inch (NYC Office of Technology and
    Innovation, NYC Open Data he6d-2qns, NYC Open Data Terms of Use):
    quoted directly (data/landcover_nyc_2017.tif, aggregated to 30 m)
    and the land-cover model's training labels
  - ESA WorldCover 2021 v200 (c) ESA WorldCover project, CC BY 4.0, the
    key the retired land-cover adapter was checked against
    (data/experimental/landcover_checks.json, tim_check.json)
  - NOAA NCEI Global Historical Climatology Network daily rainfall at
    Central Park, used to pick the rain events (U.S. public domain)

Fonts:
  Sofia Sans and Overpass Mono, each under the SIL Open Font License
  1.1 (assets/fonts/).

Logo attribution:
  The Riprap dam mark is derived from "Dam" by Chintuza, via the
  Noun Project (https://thenounproject.com/icon/dam-4516918/),
  licensed CC-BY 3.0. The original SVG embedded the attribution as
  on-canvas text; assets/logo*.svg in this repository strip the
  embedded text and carry the attribution here per the Creative
  Commons attribution requirement.

================================================================================
AI-assisted authoring
================================================================================

Portions of the source code, configuration, prompt templates, and
documentation in this repository were drafted with the assistance of large
language models. All output was reviewed and accepted by Adam Rahman, who
takes responsibility for the resulting code, claims, and reproducibility
guarantees made by this project.

This repository's initial commit consolidates work developed across many
commits in the project's working repository (msradam/riprap-nyc) between
May and July 2026, starting with the AMD Developer Cloud hackathon
(May 4-10, 2026) and continuing through the open-source release cut here.
That working history, including any "Co-Authored-By" trailers referencing
Anthropic Claude on individual commits, lives in msradam/riprap-nyc, not
in this repository's git log. This NOTICE is the artifact-level disclosure
for what ships here.
